<body>

<h1>ParsFlow MES Complete User Guide</h1>
<p><strong>Audience:</strong> Beginners, operators, supervisors, quality users, planners, management, and anyone who does not know MES yet.</p>

<div class="hero">
  <p><strong>Purpose of this guide:</strong> After reading this document, a beginner should understand what ParsFlow MES is, why it is useful, how the system modules are connected, how to log in, what each page does, what a pipe spool is, how the hardware connection works, and how to navigate the product end to end.</p>
</div>

<h2>1. Introduction to ParsFlow</h2>
<p>ParsFlow MES is a Manufacturing Execution System built to control, track, and improve shop-floor operations in real time. It combines production planning, machine visibility, operator execution, quality control, traceability, workflow approvals, RFID integration, machine connectivity, and pipe spool lifecycle tracking in one system.</p>

<h3>Why ParsFlow is better than a typical vendor solution</h3>
<table>
  <tr>
    <th>Area</th>
    <th>Why ParsFlow is stronger</th>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td>Single platform</td>
    <td>Instead of splitting planning, execution, quality, traceability, and approvals into separate tools, ParsFlow keeps them connected in one application.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td>Real-time visibility</td>
    <td>Dashboard, Andon, event streams, and telemetry make production status visible immediately rather than after manual updates.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td>Hardware flexibility</td>
    <td>The system is hardware-agnostic. RFID readers, PLC gateways, MQTT feeds, and external systems can connect through APIs and gateway services rather than vendor lock-in.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td>Traceability</td>
    <td>Each event can be tied back to machine, operator, part, spool, lot, inspection, and approval history.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td>Pipe spool specialization</td>
    <td>ParsFlow includes a dedicated spool lifecycle with weld records, NDE, pressure testing, NCR, ITP, RFID lookup, drawing register, yard tracking, and reporting.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td>User adoption</td>
    <td>The frontend is direct and visual. Even users unfamiliar with MES can follow role-based pages such as Dashboard, Planner, Operator, Quality, and Pipe Spool.</td>
  </tr>
</table>

<h2>2. What is MES?</h2>
<p>MES means Manufacturing Execution System. It sits between business planning systems and physical factory execution. In simple words, MES answers these questions:</p>
<ul>
  <li>What should be produced?</li>
  <li>Who should do the work?</li>
  <li>Which machine should run it?</li>
  <li>What happened on the floor?</li>
  <li>Did quality checks pass?</li>
  <li>Can we trace the product later?</li>
  <li>Did any machine, workflow, or approval block the job?</li>
</ul>

<div class="box">
  <p><strong>Simple beginner definition:</strong> ERP decides the business plan. MES manages the work as it actually happens in the factory.</p>
</div>

