<h2>4. How the modules are interlinked</h2>
<p>The biggest strength of ParsFlow is that pages are not isolated. Data flows from one area to another.</p>
<ol>
  <li>Planner defines the work order and schedule.</li>
  <li>Operator executes the work and creates shop-floor records.</li>
  <li>Quality checks the output and stores pass or fail decisions.</li>
  <li>Traceability uses those records to build a complete history.</li>
  <li>Dashboard summarizes all this into KPIs and alerts.</li>
  <li>Andon highlights urgent production issues.</li>
  <li>Maintenance uses telemetry and health predictions for corrective action.</li>
  <li>Workflows add approval logic when a process needs sign-off.</li>
  <li>Connectors allow machines, RFID readers, PLCs, and ERP systems to feed live data into the same system.</li>
</ol>

<div class="box">
  <p><strong>Important beginner idea:</strong> A dashboard card is usually not just a number. It is a summary of data collected from planner records, operator actions, machine events, quality checks, and hardware integration.</p>
</div>

<h2>5. How to start and log in</h2>
<h3>Run the system locally</h3>
<div class="code">cd .\mes-app
npm install
npx prisma generate
npx prisma db push
npx prisma db seed
npm run dev</div>

<p>Open <strong>http://localhost:3000</strong> in the browser.</p>

